Exercise 1: Basic Dimension Filter
Create a Sales by Sub-Category bar chart.
- Apply a Region filter.
- Display only the West region.
- Change the selection to East, Central, and South.
- Observe how the chart changes.

Exercise 2: Multiple Values Filter
- Create a Sales by Category chart.
- Filter the Segment field.
- Select Consumer and Corporate.
- Test single-value and multiple-value selection controls.

Exercise 3: Wildcard Filter
- Use the Product Name field.
- Create a filter using the Wildcard Match option.
- Practise Contains, Starts With, Ends With, and Exact Match.
- Compare the products displayed for different patterns.
Exercise 4: Condition Filter
- Filter Sub-Category based on a condition.
- Create a condition based on aggregated Sales.
- Create another condition using aggregated Profit.
- Compare the categories or sub-categories that remain.
Exercise 5: Show Filter Card
- Create a Sales by State chart.
- Display Region and Category filters.
- Change the filters interactively.
- Configure the filter cards to suit the worksheet.

Section 2 — Measure Filters
Exercises 6–9
Exercise 6: SUM(Sales) Filter
Create a Sales by Sub-Category chart.
- Filter using SUM(Sales).
- Practise a range of sales values.
- Compare the results with different minimum and maximum values.

Exercise 7: Profit Filter
- Create a Category and Sub-Category view.
- Filter using SUM(Profit).
- Investigate positive and negative profit values.
- Compare the displayed categories before and after filtering.

Exercise 8: Measure Filter — At Least and At Most
- Create a Sales by Product Name chart.
- Practise At Least, At Most, and Range of Values.
- Observe which products remain when the limits change.

Exercise 9: Quantity and Discount Filters
- Create a view showing Sales, Quantity, and Discount.
- Apply filters to Quantity and Discount.
- Test different ranges and compare the resulting view.


Section 3 — Date Filters
Exercises 10–13
Exercise 10: Discrete Date Filter
- Create a monthly Sales chart using Order Date.
- Practise selecting individual years, quarters, and months.
- Compare discrete date selections with continuous dates.

Exercise 11: Range of Dates
- Create a Sales trend chart.
- Apply a range filter to Order Date.
- Restrict the view to a selected start and end date.
- Change the date range and observe the trend.

Exercise 12: Relative Date Filter
- Apply a relative date filter to Order Date.
- Practise the last, current, and previous date periods available.
- Compare the results for days, months, quarters, and years.

Exercise 13: Date and Region Combination
- Create a monthly Sales chart.
- Add Order Date and Region filters.
- Select different date periods and regions.
- Explain how the combined filters affect the trend.


Section 4 — Top N, Condition and Context Filters
Exercises 14–18
Exercise 14: Top 10 Products by Sales
- Create a Product Name filter.
- Configure it to display the Top 10 products by Sales.
- Change the number from 10 to 5 and then 15.
- Repeat the exercise using Profit.

Exercise 15: Bottom 10 Products
- Find the Bottom 10 products by Sales.
- Repeat using Profit.
- Compare the results and identify products with negative profit.

Exercise 16: Conditional Filter
- Filter Sub-Category using a field condition.
- Create a second condition using a formula.
- Compare the results when the condition changes.

Exercise 17: Context Filter
- Create a Top 5 Sub-Category by Sales view.
- Apply Region as a normal filter.
- Repeat after adding Region to context.
- Record the categories displayed in each case.
- Explain why the results differ or remain the same.

Exercise 18: Multiple Filters and Order of Operations
- Combine Region, Category, and Top N filters.
- Change the order in which you configure the filters.
- Add and remove context from selected filters.
- Document how the final results change.


Section 5 — Extract and Data Source Filters
Exercises 19–21
Exercise 19: Extract Filter
- Connect to Sample Superstore.
- Configure an extract filter using Order Date.
- Create an extract containing a selected date period.
- Compare the available data with the original full dataset.

Exercise 20: Data Source Filter
- Open the Data Source page.
- Add a data source filter on Region.
- Create a worksheet using the filtered source.
- Examine the available regions and compare them with the original source.

Exercise 21: Compare Filter Levels
- Create separate copies of your workbook.
- Apply an extract filter in one copy.
- Apply a data source filter in another.
- Compare the data available to the worksheets and the effect on the workbook.


Section 6 — Table Calculation Filters
Exercises 22–24
Exercise 22: Rank Filter
- Create a Sales by Sub-Category chart.
- Create a rank table calculation using Sales.
- Filter the view to show only the first five ranks.
- Change the calculation direction and observe the results.

Exercise 23: Running Total Filter
- Create a monthly Sales chart.
- Add a running total table calculation.
- Apply a table calculation filter.
- Observe how the displayed marks change.

Exercise 24: Table Calculation vs Top N Filter
- Create two worksheets showing Sales by Sub-Category.
- Apply a Top 5 dimension filter to one worksheet.
- Apply a rank-based table calculation filter to the other.
- Compare the results when Region is filtered normally and when it is added to context.

